From 2b47399c985e9183a8ce93d0d9580d6983228535 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:33:42 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: softisp: Set VBLANK from the frame duration limits

The software ISP IPA wrote only exposure and gain, so FrameDurationLimits
had no effect on the frame rate: a sensor mode ran at its default frame
length. On the ov13b10 the 2x2 binned modes run at 60 fps, and an Android
camera HAL asking for 30 fps got 60.

Write VBLANK too. In the simple pipeline add VBLANK to the delayed
controls as a priority write, and without a frameStart signal write it
ahead of the other controls, as sensor drivers widen the exposure range
for a longer frame. On sensors whose VBLANK delay exceeds the exposure
and gain delays this raises the delayed controls' maximum delay.

Add a setsFrameLength flag to the AGC configuration, which the soft ISP
sets. With it:
- the AGC computes vblank for the exposure time of the same frame
  context, which the IPA writes together with it: the minimum frame
  duration rounded up to whole lines, or the exposure time if that is
  longer. The FrameDuration metadata then matches the frame length
  written, also for frames without statistics;
- the frame duration limits default to the sensor's default frame
  length. The AGC otherwise defaults them to the shortest and longest
  frames the VBLANK range allows, and writing VBLANK from that would run
  a sensor whose minimum VBLANK is below its default faster than before,
  at a rate that varies with the exposure time.
Other IPAs keep the old behaviour.

With an ov13b10 and a command-line client, a 2096x1560 stream (2104x1560
mode) runs at 59.6 fps without limits, and gives 33.34 ms frames (3175
lines) with a 33.3 ms minimum; a full-size stream gives 33.56 ms, the
mode's minimum.

Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
